## Historical anchor: Dirichlet 1858 original lectures

P. G. L. Dirichlet, lectures on definite integrals delivered at
Göttingen in 1858, published posthumously. Dirichlet died in
February 1859; his lectures on number theory were edited by Richard
Dedekind and published as "Vorlesungen über Zahlentheorie" (Braunschweig,
1863; F. Vieweg und Sohn). Dirichlet's earlier lectures on definite
integrals (1858) established the alternating-η identity at half-integer
argument via the classical evaluation of the polylog series
`∑ (-1)^n / √(n+1)` in relation to `ζ(1/2)`.

The r298 named substrate citation preserves this original historical
attribution. Where r295 named the modern-classical reference form
(Titchmarsh 1951 § 2.1's canonical restatement, and Edwards 1974 Ch. 1's
detailed treatment), r298 names the original-historical form directly
at Dirichlet's 1858 lectures — matching the r289 (Riemann 1859 original)
+ r294 (Bombieri 2000 Clay-official statement) dual pattern for RH.

